<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">James Clerk Maxwell</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1831">1831</a>–<a href="/years/1879">1879</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">James Clerk Maxwell was a Scottish physicist whose equations, published in the 1860s, unified electricity, magnetism and light in a single theory of the electromagnetic field. He also made fundamental contributions to the kinetic theory of gases and to colour <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/photography">photography</a>, and became the first Cavendish Professor of Physics at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambridge">Cambridge</a> in 1871.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/james-clerk-maxwell.jpg" alt="James Clerk Maxwell" width="320" height="400" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:James-Clerk-Maxwell-1831-1879.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>James Clerk Maxwell was born on 13 June 1831 at 14 India Street, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edinburgh">Edinburgh</a>, the son of John Clerk Maxwell of Middlebie, an advocate, and Frances Cay. His mother took charge of his early education, and at eight he could recite long passages of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-milton">John Milton</a> and the whole of the 119th psalm. She died of abdominal cancer in December 1839. On 12 February 1842 his father took him to Robert Davidson's demonstration of electric propulsion and magnetic force. He studied at the University of Edinburgh from 1847 and discovered photoelasticity there. In October 1850 he went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambridge">Cambridge</a> and graduated in 1854 from Trinity.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 1856 Maxwell became head of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">department</a> of natural philosophy at Marischal College in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aberdeen">Aberdeen</a>. He studied the rings of Saturn and correctly proposed that they were made of numerous small particles, for which he won the Adams Prize in 1859. At King's College London, from 1860 to 1865, he did what was probably the most productive work of his career: he won the Rumford Medal in 1860 for his work on colour, was elected to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-society">Royal Society</a> in 1861. Around 1862 he calculated that an electromagnetic field travels at about the speed of light, and his paper A Dynamical Theory of the Electromagnetic Field of 1865 showed that electric and magnetic fields travel through space as waves.</p><p>He also worked on the kinetic theory of gases, first deriving the Maxwell–Boltzmann distribution. In 1871 he came back to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambridge">Cambridge</a> as the first Cavendish Professor of Physics and oversaw the construction of the Cavendish Laboratory.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In April 1879 Maxwell began to have difficulty swallowing, the first symptom of his fatal illness. He died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambridge">Cambridge</a> of abdominal cancer on 5 November 1879, aged 48, the same age and of the same type of cancer as his mother. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albert-einstein">Albert Einstein</a> described his work on the centenary of Maxwell's birth as the most profound and fruitful that physics had experienced since the time of Newton, and when told in 1922 that he stood on Newton's shoulders he replied that he stood on those of Maxwell.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “James Clerk Maxwell” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Science and thought flourished: Charles Darwin’s On the Origin of Species (1859), the work of Michael Faraday and <b>James Clerk Maxwell</b> on electromagnetism, and the economics of David Ricardo and John Stuart Mill.</blockquote><a href="/empire/united-kingdom-of-great-britain-and-ireland">United Kingdom of Great Britain and Ireland · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edinburgh">Edinburgh</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambridge">Cambridge</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=55.95,-3.19&amp;year=1862" aria-label="Edinburgh on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4481 258 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of James Clerk Maxwell"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4931 528h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4931 528h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=55.95,-3.19&amp;year=1862">What was here in 1862? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
